rk3588-camera-dkms (0.1.4-1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * imx708: support four data lanes, and faster mode timings for links with
    more bandwidth, taken from Raspberry Pi's imx708 link frequency changes
    (raspberrypi/linux #7605): each mode lists a row per link frequency (447,
    549, 750, 960 MHz) with a timing for two lanes and one for four. 4608x2592 runs
    at 27 fps (four lanes at 450 MHz), 30 fps (four lanes at 549 MHz, two lanes at 960 MHz), 22.6 fps (two
    lanes, 750 MHz) and 45 fps (four lanes, 750 MHz); 2304x1296 reaches
    120 fps (four lanes, 549 MHz) and 179 fps (750 MHz). The PLL registers
    are static values per timing and per link frequency (447, 450, 453, 549,
    750, 960 MHz). Lane rates above 1.5 Gbps warn at probe: deskew
    calibration is not enabled.
  * Build a replacement imx415 module: the mainline driver (v7.3-rc4) plus a
    2376 Mbps lane rate, for 3864x2192 at about 90 fps on four lanes with a
    37.125 MHz sensor clock. It installs under updates/dkms, which depmod
    prefers over Ubuntu's module. The 720, 891, 1440 Mbps modes are unchanged.
  * imx415: allow 2376 Mbps with a 24 MHz clock, a combination outside the
    data sheet, when the module is loaded with allow_24mhz_2376=1. It is off by
    default and the driver warns when it is used. The mode defaults to 151
    lines of vertical blanking (90.0 fps). Tried on one Arducam B0569 module.
  * imx415: lowering VBLANK below a mode's default left the exposure range
    unchanged, so an exposure was accepted that put SHR0 below its minimum
    and the image came out almost black. Fix the range update, and clamp the
    exposure to VMAX - 8 when SHR0 is written.
  * imx415: report the pixel array for the native size selection target too.
    libcamera logs an error about the missing target every time it opens the
    camera.
  * Bind rockchip,rk3588-isp-unite and run both ISP cores on one camera.
    Each core takes half of the frame (cut at half the width rounded up to
    16 pixels, so that the right core's output starts on a 16 byte boundary)
    plus 128 overlap columns on its inner side: it reads its part from memory
    (the right core starts 128 pixels before the cut), writes its part of
    each output line, and its dual crop removes the overlap. Interrupts
    follow the right core and the left core's flags are cleared separately,
    but a frame is handled only once both cores have flagged its end, so a
    buffer is never handed on with part of it still being written. The AE,
    histogram and AWB windows are split between the cores (each measures
    half of the frame with half-width cells) and their results are put back
    together for the IPA. Scaling the output and horizontal cropping are not
    supported in this mode, which is for streams that do not fit on one core,
    at the sensor's size; the driver rejects a scaled main-path output.
  * rkisp2: the check that rejects a scaled main-path output in unite mode
    called the ISP subdev's get_fmt with a NULL state. A subdev with an active
    state expects the caller to pass it; Ubuntu's 7.3.0-9 tolerates the NULL,
    other kernels may not. Pass the active state.
  * rkisp2: in unite mode, setting the main path's format to a size other than
    the ISP's input returns the unscaled size. The rejection at stream start
    only happens at the first buffer queue, where libcamera logs the error and
    waits for frames forever; now the configure step fails at once.
  * rkisp2: pad the lines of the processed output to 64 bytes. A 3864 pixel
    wide UYVY frame has 7728 bytes per line, and Mesa's Vulkan WSI refuses to
    present a buffer with a pitch that is not aligned (one error per frame in
    a GTK dmabuf preview of the IMX415). Sizes that already are aligned do not
    change.

 -- Peyton Howe <peyton@localhost>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

rk3588-camera-dkms (0.1.3-1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Replace Ubuntu's rockchip-cif with rkcif v7.3 plus a fixed stream stop.
    It now stops the capture at a frame end before stopping the source,
    and frees the buffers and dummy buffer only after the DMA is idle.
    The interrupt handler no longer takes the subdev state mutex. With the
    stock driver, repeated start/stop hung a ROCK 5B+ within a few cycles.
  * rkcif: on RK3588 skip the CRU reset in runtime suspend, which hung a
    ROCK 5B+ silently a few dozen streams in, and soft-reset the MIPI host's
    capture logic when a stream stops instead, as Rockchip's driver does on
    RK3588. The soft reset also clears the frame address the DMA would keep
    from the previous stream. Other SoCs keep the CRU reset.
  * Build the DCPHY replacement module (phy-rockchip-samsung-dcphy, DCPHY RX
    v5), which adds CSI reception to the SoC's MIPI DCPHYs. It installs under
    updates/dkms, which depmod prefers over Ubuntu's transmitter-only module.
    dcphy-compat.h supplies PHY_DIRECTION_TX/PHY_DIRECTION_RX for stock headers.
  * After installing, run depmod and rebuild that kernel's initrd. The initrd
    otherwise keeps the stock DCPHY module, which loads first and rejects the
    receiver's second phy cell, so the CSI host never binds.

 -- Peyton Howe <peyton@localhost>  Sun, 18 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0000

rk3588-camera-dkms (0.1.2-1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Only recommend linux-headers-generic. Depending on it made
    apt-get -f install pull in the archive's newest kernel alongside the
    ISO's; the image build now installs headers for each installed kernel.

